## Why This Is Asked
Designing adaptive models under load tests real-time orchestration and governance in GCP. It probes latency budgeting, feature-store usage, and rollback mechanics.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-model online/offline design
- Vertex AI Endpoints traffic splitting
- Real-time feature engineering with Pub/Sub/Dataflow/Feature Store
- Drift and latency monitoring, rollback via registry pinning
- Data Catalog lineage for auditability

## Code Example
```javascript
function chooseModel(latency, budget){
  if (latency <= 100 && budget > 0){
    return 'lightweight';
  }
  return 'heavy';
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design a scalable, compliant, and observable MLOps workflow that supports multi-tenant isolation while meeting real-time processing requirements and governance standards.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant architecture with per-tenant resource isolation
- Real-time stream processing with PII redaction at ingestion
- Feature store management for tenant-specific model training
- Canary deployment strategies for safe model rollouts
- Model explainability and drift detection with automated rollback
- Comprehensive data lineage and governance tracking
- Cost management controls at the tenant level
- Late-arrival data handling within specified time windows

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: canary deployment decision logic
if (tenantDrift[tenant] < DRIFT_THRESHOLD) {
  promoteCanaryToProduction(tenant);
} else {
  rollbackToStableModel(tenant);
  triggerAlert(tenant, 'Model drift detected');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle schema evolution across tenants?
- What strategies would you use for optimizing costs in a multi-tenant environment?
- How would you ensure data consistency when processing late-arriving events?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a modular, governance-minded pipeline that handles multilingual data and targeted evaluation without overcomplication. It emphasizes per-language data management, explainability, and rollback—common beginner-friendly MLOps concerns.

## Key Concepts
- Language-based routing and partitioning
- Per-language training subsets in Vertex AI
- Unified endpoint with traffic-splitting by language
- Explainable AI per language
- Drift monitoring and simple rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-configuration sketch for per-language training
const trainingPlan = {
  languages: ['en','es','de'],
  schedule: 'weekly',
  endpoint: {
    type: 'trafficSplit',
    rules: { en: 0.5, es: 0.3, de: 0.2 }
  }
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you add new languages with minimal downtime?
- How would you validate that explainability insights align with human judgments?
